(20!\\) ends in four zeros. Given the last 18 digits of \\(20!\\) are 432902008176640000, this confirms the last four digits are zeros.\n\nNext, to find the first digit of \\(20!\\), we can use an approximation method involving logarithms. We approximate the logarithm of \\(20!\\):\n\n\\[\n\\log_{10}(20!) \\approx \\sum_{k=1}^{20} \\log_{10}(k)\n\\]\n\nUsing a calculator or logarithm tables, we compute:\n\n\\[\n\\log_{10}(1) + \\log_{10}(2) + \\cdots + \\log_{10}(20)\n\\]\n\nSumming these values:\n\n\\[\n\\log_{10}(20!) \\approx 18.38\n\\]\n\nThis implies:\n\n\\[\n20! \\approx 10^{18.38} = 10^{0.38} \\times 10^{18}\n\\]\n\nWe calculate \\(10^{0.38}\\) using a calculator:\n\n\\[\n10^{0.38} \\approx 2.4\n\\]\n\nThus,\n\n\\[\n20! \\approx 2.4 \\times 10^{18}\n\\]\n\nGiven the trailing zeros and the last 18 digits, we can confirm that the first digit of \\(20!\\) must be \\(2\\).\n\nTherefore, the first digit of \\(20!\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{2}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"Given that \\(20!\\) has 19 digits, and the last 18 digits are 432902008176640000, we aim to find the first digit. \n\nFirst, we recognize that \\(20!\\) includes multiples of \\(2\\) and \\(5\\), which contribute to trailing zeros. Counting the number of trailing zeros in \\(20!\\) involves counting factors of \\(10\\), which are products of \\(2\\) and \\(5\\). Since there are more factors of \\(2\\) than \\(5\\), the number of trailing zeros is determined by the number of factors of \\(5\\):\n\n- In \\(20!\\), the factors of \\(5\\) occur in \\(5, 10, 15,\\) and \\(20\\), giving us four factors of \\(5\\).\n\nThis means \\(20!\\) ends in four zeros. Given the last 18 digits of \\(20!\\) are 432902008176640000, this confirms the last four digits are zeros.\n\nNext, to find the first digit of \\(20!\\), we can use an approximation method involving logarithms. We approximate the logarithm of \\(20!\\):\n\n\\[\n\\log_{10}(20!) \\approx \\sum_{k=1}^{20} \\log_{10}(k)\n\\]\n\nUsing a calculator or logarithm tables, we compute:\n\n\\[\n\\log_{10}(1) + \\log_{10}(2) + \\cdots + \\log_{10}(20)\n\\]\n\nSumming these values:\n\n\\[\n\\log_{10}(20!) \\approx 18.38\n\\]\n\nThis implies:\n\n\\[\n20! \\approx 10^{18.38} = 10^{0.38} \\times 10^{18}\n\\]\n\nWe calculate \\(10^{0.38}\\) using a calculator:\n\n\\[\n10^{0.38} \\approx 2.4\n\\]\n\nThus,\n\n\\[\n20! \\approx 2.4 \\times 10^{18}\n\\]\n\nGiven the trailing zeros and the last 18 digits, we can confirm that the first digit of \\(20!\\) must be \\(2\\).\n\nTherefore, the first digit of \\(20!\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}\n\\]","id":"number_theory:445"}
